1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,291
This is team 45's ICC Rankings project. It is a MySQL 8
database with a Python Flask backend, and the web

2
00:00:07,291 --> 00:00:13,262
interface is modelled on the I C C rankings website. On the
right is the mysql client.

3
00:00:13,862 --> 00:00:20,733
All data was taken from the I C C website: ranking tables at
three dates, player profiles, and real match

4
00:00:20,733 --> 00:00:24,660
scorecards. The client shows how many rows each table holds.

5
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:28,843
The main pages are team rankings, player rankings,

6
00:00:29,443 --> 00:00:31,413
matches with scorecards,

7
00:00:32,013 --> 00:00:34,302
and player and team profiles.

8
00:00:34,902 --> 00:00:45,161
Query one, team rankings. Men's Test: South Africa, 23
matches, 2860 points, rating 124, exactly as on the I C

9
00:00:45,161 --> 00:00:52,343
C site. Rating is points divided by matches, and rank uses a
window function.

10
00:00:52,943 --> 00:00:58,746
The rank date filter shows an earlier snapshot, with
movement arrows computed against the previous table.

11
00:00:59,346 --> 00:01:03,420
The tabs switch gender and format, here women's T20I.

12
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:10,064
Query two, player rankings. Men's ODI batting, with Shubman
Gill on top.

13
00:01:10,664 --> 00:01:16,686
The all rounder table is derived in SQL as batting points
times bowling points divided by a thousand, which

14
00:01:16,686 --> 00:01:18,262
matches the website exactly.

15
00:01:18,862 --> 00:01:24,260
Query three, search. Typing k o h finds Kohli by partial
name.

16
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:30,702
Searching keeper finds players by role, and teams are
matched by name or code with their current format

17
00:01:30,702 --> 00:01:31,099
status.

18
00:01:31,699 --> 00:01:39,357
Query four, the player profile. Age is derived from date of
birth, with styles, current rank in every table,

19
00:01:39,957 --> 00:01:45,259
rating history with the change from the previous snapshot,
and career runs and wickets summed from the

20
00:01:45,259 --> 00:01:45,830
scorecards.

21
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:53,783
Query five, the team profile for India: format status
history, win and loss record, and top performers.

22
00:01:54,383 --> 00:01:59,357
It also shows the rating trend in each format, with the
change between snapshots.

23
00:01:59,957 --> 00:02:06,665
Query six, matches. Filter by format, status and gender,
here completed ODIs.

24
00:02:07,265 --> 00:02:12,929
A match page shows both teams, the venue, the result and
every player's batting and bowling figures.

25
00:02:13,529 --> 00:02:21,205
Query seven, head to head. India against New Zealand: wins,
losses and draws counted with aggregates, and the

26
00:02:21,205 --> 00:02:22,332
list of matches.

27
00:02:22,932 --> 00:02:30,220
Query eight, player comparison. Gill and Kohli side by side,
latest ratings per format and recorded runs.

28
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:35,438
Query nine, the players who improved most between two chosen
rank dates.

29
00:02:36,038 --> 00:02:43,083
Query ten, a series report: its matches, top run scorers and
wicket takers, and each team's rating before and

30
00:02:43,083 --> 00:02:44,181
after the series.

31
00:02:44,781 --> 00:02:51,915
Query eleven, statistics: the highest, lowest and average
team rating per table and date, teams with no recent

32
00:02:51,915 --> 00:02:54,574
match, and ranked teams per I C C member.

33
00:02:55,174 --> 00:03:01,625
Query twelve, analyst workload: rating rows published per
user per month, grouped under their supervisor using

34
00:03:01,625 --> 00:03:03,267
the recursive user relation.

35
00:03:03,867 --> 00:03:11,870
Now the updates. First, create. Ireland has no Test rating
yet: the query returns an empty set.

36
00:03:12,470 --> 00:03:18,073
On Ireland's page we publish a Test snapshot dated today, 10
matches and 300 points.

37
00:03:18,673 --> 00:03:22,757
The app confirms it, and re-running the same query shows the
new row.

38
00:03:23,357 --> 00:03:26,171
Ireland now appears in the men's Test table.

39
00:03:26,771 --> 00:03:33,400
Second create: a player rating for Abhishek Sharma. Here are
his current T20I batting ratings.

40
00:03:33,1000 --> 00:03:41,028
A rating of 1500 is rejected by the check constraint,
because ratings must be between 0 and 1000.

41
00:03:41,628 --> 00:03:46,916
With 891 the insert succeeds, and the same query now shows
today's row.

42
00:03:47,516 --> 00:03:53,355
Third create: adding a player. Player 900001 does not exist
yet.

43
00:03:53,955 --> 00:03:59,268
If the gender does not match the team, a trigger rejects it
and the whole transaction rolls back.

44
00:03:59,868 --> 00:04:05,152
With the correct gender, the player, the role and the team
spell are inserted in one transaction.

45
00:04:05,752 --> 00:04:12,260
Now the updates. India won this match against New Zealand,
and neither team's rating needs recomputing.

46
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:17,828
Setting both teams to won is rejected by a trigger, because
the results must agree.

47
00:04:18,428 --> 00:04:25,158
A consistent change succeeds. The results are swapped, and
the trigger flags both teams' latest ratings for

48
00:04:25,158 --> 00:04:25,786
recompute.

49
00:04:26,386 --> 00:04:29,790
The rankings table now shows the needs recompute badge.

50
00:04:30,390 --> 00:04:34,554
Retiring a player: Abhishek Sharma has no retired formats.

51
00:04:35,154 --> 00:04:40,183
We retire him from T20Is today, and the query shows the new
retirement row.

52
00:04:40,783 --> 00:04:46,046
He disappears from the current T20I batting table, while his
rating history is kept.

53
00:04:46,646 --> 00:04:51,215
Changing a team: the new player currently represents India
with an open spell.

54
00:04:51,815 --> 00:04:58,028
After moving him to Nepal, the old spell is closed with an
end date and a new spell is opened, atomically.

55
00:04:58,628 --> 00:05:06,763
Ending a format status: Zimbabwe currently holds Test
status, and its earlier 1992 to 2006 period is kept as

56
00:05:06,763 --> 00:05:07,366
history.

57
00:05:07,966 --> 00:05:13,139
After ending it today, the end date is set and the latest
Test rating is flagged for recompute.

58
00:05:13,739 --> 00:05:19,638
Publishing a new Zimbabwe Test rating is now rejected,
because the team no longer holds the status.

59
00:05:20,238 --> 00:05:24,956
Correcting a snapshot: India's ODI rating is still flagged
for recompute.

60
00:05:25,556 --> 00:05:29,600
After saving the corrected points, the update clears the
flag.

61
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:40,712
Now the deletes. Match 274664 is scheduled, and match 271652
is completed.

62
00:05:41,312 --> 00:05:43,771
Deleting the scheduled match succeeds.

63
00:05:44,371 --> 00:05:49,762
Deleting the completed match is refused by a trigger. The
query shows the scheduled match and its

64
00:05:49,762 --> 00:05:52,819
participations are gone, and the completed one remains.

65
00:05:53,419 --> 00:05:57,987
Deleting a rating snapshot: here is the Ireland Test row
created earlier.

66
00:05:58,587 --> 00:06:02,261
After deleting it, the same query returns an empty set.

67
00:06:02,861 --> 00:06:08,374
Deleting Shubman Gill is refused, because foreign keys
restrict deleting a player with ratings and

68
00:06:08,374 --> 00:06:09,049
appearances.

69
00:06:09,649 --> 00:06:12,208
The newly added player has no history.

70
00:06:12,808 --> 00:06:19,456
So the delete succeeds, and his role and team spells cascade
with him. The query returns an empty set.

71
00:06:20,056 --> 00:06:26,983
Every query and update is raw parameterised SQL in queries
dot py, run through PyMySQL, and constraint

72
00:06:26,983 --> 00:06:30,379
violations are shown as clear messages. Thank you.
